  coach: { SR: 'The source range tripped at 100,000 counts per second. During a startup, block the source range as soon as P-6 comes in (intermediate range above 1E-10 A), and keep the startup rate under 1 decade per minute.', IR: 'The intermediate range trip (25%) came in: power climbed too fast at low power. Withdraw rods in short bursts and let the startup rate settle between them.', PRL: 'Power passed 25% before the low-setpoint trip was blocked. Block it (P-10) once two power range channels read above 10%, or hold power lower.', PRH: 'Power passed 109%. Raise load slowly, keep Tavg on Tref, and only use rods in AUTO when the plant is steady.', PZRPL: 'Pressuriser pressure fell to 134 bar. Find what was taking pressure away: a PORV or spray valve open, a leak, or a fast cooldown. Shut the block valve, stop the sprays, check the heaters are on.', PZRPH: 'Pressuriser pressure reached 166 bar, usually because the plant heated up faster than the sprays could cope (a turbine trip or load rejection). Cut power quickly and check sprays and PORVs are in AUTO.', PZRL: 'Pressuriser level hit 92%: charging was putting in more than letdown took out, or the coolant heated up. Check the charging controller and Tavg.', LOFLOW: 'Reactor coolant flow fell because a coolant pump stopped. Check the power to its board and its bearing cooling (CCW).', SGLL: 'A steam generator level fell to 17%: feed didn’t keep up with steam. Watch feed flow against steam flow on board 7, take the feed valve to MANUAL early, and cut turbine load fast if a feed pump is lost.', OTDT: 'Power and temperature were too high together, eating into the margin to boiling on the fuel. Keep Tavg on Tref and avoid big axial flux swings.', OPDT: 'Power and temperature were too high together, eating into the margin to boiling on the fuel. Keep Tavg on Tref and avoid big axial flux swings.', TT: 'The turbine tripped above 50% power, which trips the reactor. Find why the turbine tripped: condenser vacuum, overspeed or a protection signal.', SI: 'Safety injection actuated, which trips the reactor. Something lost pressure fast: a LOCA, a steam line break or a stuck PORV.', MAN: 'You tripped the reactor by hand. That’s right when an automatic trip is coming anyway or the plant is out of control. Look at the graphs: could you have caught it earlier?', MG: 'The automatic trip failed (ATWS) and the rods only went in when the MG sets were de-energised. Every second counts: the sooner the rods go in, the lower the peak pressure.', SEIS: 'The earthquake triggered the seismic trip. That’s the plant doing its job: the real test was what came next.' },
